TYPES OF MOBILE INTERNET NETWORKS

2G  Second Generation
    Speed: 100-300 kbps
    First mobile data (GSM/CDMA era)

3G  Third Generation
    Speed: 1-3 Mbps
    First mobile broadband (UMTS/HSPA)

4G  Fourth Generation
    Speed: 5-100 Mbps
    Faster + more reliable (LTE foundation)

4G LTE  Enhanced 4G
    Speed: 10-150 Mbps
    Improved latency and capacity over basic 4G

5G  Fifth Generation
    Speed: 1-10 Gbps
    Ultra-fast + very low latency (mmWave + sub-6)

TYPES OF MOBILE INTERNET NETWORKS

2G  SECOND GENERATION
Speed: 100-300 kbps
Role: first mobile data
Tech: GSM / GPRS / EDGE
Use: SMS, basic web, low-res media

3G  THIRD GENERATION
Speed: 1-3 Mbps
Role: first mobile broadband
Tech: UMTS / HSPA
Use: video calls, mobile browsing, apps

4G  FOURTH GENERATION
Speed: 5-100 Mbps
Role: faster, more reliable packet data
Tech: LTE / WiMAX-class IP networks
Use: HD streaming, maps, always-on apps

4G LTE  ENHANCED 4G
Speed: 10-150 Mbps
Role: higher-efficiency 4G
Tech: LTE / LTE-Advanced
Use: smoother video, lower delay than early 4G

5G  FIFTH GENERATION
Speed: 1-10 Gbps peak
Role: ultra-fast, low-latency connectivity
Tech: NR (New Radio), massive MIMO
Use: 4K/8K video, cloud gaming, IoT, AR/VR
Latency: often ~1-10 ms in good conditions
